#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12562" :question/problem "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198077 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12563" :question/problem "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/0d/0de380d24ed675cff86b219a2a4a337699d68e168fc875475158523022915b0c.svg) Give your answer in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/61/61c5a4204c4ad078bd036eee7770e28faf4741bedb274a54d08d54dae43fbc68.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/ab/ab531b6a5468a0ddb8cf46a42300f19cc6406c94c011ae814d60af24f66e2aa0.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/3d/3dbb0d517f6875d4a0601ba284878cb60ac0b615491564ae9458c34c0f3de403.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/3c/3cbbec95c6327e77a53bdeea5de4a93481ca9f4164ebae0cb99bf28468513a12.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/31/31305b4a4a27141bae70eabbb04acaf98928c275f8efec37ef8a869c7599a2f3.svg) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/a7/a7a5f8961c77ad8a606ce57a543d5606b834061f3a0777b074a5711dcc9a4529.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/27/276fd591c58ea0bd80eb0d3c236ade53fcd1d38864ccd650873cf052561b7586.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/06/06c98e6248f7fdb303c04772921b0423f8e2f88450d330c295bc3a72846ef858.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/3a/3ac30d1603a502de1404a1888f7c410d22ae93b8b47db6694734a09634a43954.svg) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/af/afd14fa251e88edb40ea71b70c56db5a03ec83d906608930e2b3ba3ba9c9fb0b.svg)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/1a/1af733b173aa4a358cd8c0b5c2754431be57887e7c43b0aa98125deefdecc4ad.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e54e1c9-205f-5069-a26e-144f61699b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278873} {:answer/id #uuid "53a1bca1-f8d6-5bc4-914c-05bf62ee4e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279353} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae60956-68d4-52a5-a560-be8be1ee1d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279431} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4c4625-0875-5288-88dc-8eec11112601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280648} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d11b4-8aaf-5097-aa76-39368cc3df6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e54e1c9-205f-5069-a26e-144f61699b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278873} :answer-field/id #uuid "b152db49-e069-50cf-bae6-a47ce8602ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a23d317-8ce9-5c6e-8d7d-b18f5386617b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162992" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{x-2y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+x+a\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c1228e-2050-56a2-b557-e042a94e13ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278617} {:answer/id #uuid "65bcf28f-9665-53c8-ad9d-52494b8f71a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279517} {:answer/id #uuid "867433f2-e62a-5149-8f65-89ea1338e1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279791} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ef3ec5-904b-5bac-aaf1-4b133a5204b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280159} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c774d0-0b8d-5b1a-aac1-8ea3a6aa6f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867433f2-e62a-5149-8f65-89ea1338e1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279791} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b371790-c15b-5ab7-8f10-3eae5d762331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40019177-315b-5f8c-afeb-29b9f1c22ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163088" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{xy},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+bxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12776ee4-bf66-52be-b94e-6da855692143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278760} {:answer/id #uuid "2992873e-5c72-56af-9b65-d007f9624836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278958} {:answer/id #uuid "3d837bf8-64a8-5ba8-b11f-d1e54f4538d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279133} {:answer/id #uuid "b756c24d-21f1-5fd7-825b-a181399c4bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280201} {:answer/id #uuid "d732b216-6686-5d3d-b266-b392c5e5f514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b756c24d-21f1-5fd7-825b-a181399c4bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280201} :answer-field/id #uuid "9241f691-955a-5b21-a8a2-fb377b58fa65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f950d98b-8d67-594b-b76b-e7454ed0e566" :question/math-academy-id "q-163095" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+b{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2(1+2{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1{x}^{2}+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+1=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d21ce0-309e-51b2-b71a-317f7673986c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278656} {:answer/id #uuid "3af19d5c-b69f-565b-999b-0e2e1458f0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279115} {:answer/id #uuid "9e74d61d-582b-5a16-ab29-864d8dbb15a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279997} {:answer/id #uuid "abf5d547-610e-59e8-93d7-5d6cc804f793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280116} {:answer/id #uuid "cce87375-9ff6-5f1f-b864-2b4877a1fcfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e74d61d-582b-5a16-ab29-864d8dbb15a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279997} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d1b0f9-cfc4-562a-82ed-b91bc6be2331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7c75fe7-9dcd-544f-9c7d-b36cf94f46b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163102" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y & \\frac{x}{y}+x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}+1 & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n2+(-1)+(x-1)=x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5416260a-3d53-53a5-af7e-41639a604a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279355} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec951df-9880-5a93-9db3-2fe43c2f9ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279462} {:answer/id #uuid "65693471-a7be-5477-a1f7-2e187e93701d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279512} {:answer/id #uuid "b54c50b5-05e7-51f8-8c7c-310987128e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280186} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ed62de-42a6-5602-9391-b7944f4315bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec951df-9880-5a93-9db3-2fe43c2f9ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279462} :answer-field/id #uuid "2384f8d6-605a-5711-a7e5-c29c9b3f7d66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b07db089-383d-5673-822f-6a5d7846d04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163104" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y)^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(x-y)^{2} & -3(x-y)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}6(x-y) & -6(x-y) \\\\ -6(x-y) & 6(x-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\nx+6+(y-1)=x+y+5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b8dc7b-9ca0-57e4-95cf-ce295303bd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432337} {:answer/id #uuid "426ee39a-6753-5cfc-b697-f79544216277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432344} {:answer/id #uuid "614bbaef-7919-554f-80d5-216b1c9e41ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432349} {:answer/id #uuid "976864ab-946d-5ed5-a93b-3f163e2eea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432352} {:answer/id #uuid "f88f206b-7ef5-5e41-82c9-dd42ea25469f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f88f206b-7ef5-5e41-82c9-dd42ea25469f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432363} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d73795-71d5-5b5f-aef4-f1f6186ed7de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2c36de6-e7f2-52de-808d-79c266cdf722" :question/math-academy-id "q-163109" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y+1},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+ax-y+{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a163f00-423b-57e8-ae46-b60f111d48a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278964} {:answer/id #uuid "3582f22b-3311-5a03-87b8-3529a0043ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279060} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc1b6c6-2219-5b6b-b6fb-06d4eeaed11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279269} {:answer/id #uuid "7be2b338-9c19-55aa-9af1-1128dd120960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279698} {:answer/id #uuid "dd278181-66cd-5b8a-9eb0-5c8613158328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a163f00-423b-57e8-ae46-b60f111d48a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278964} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6589ef-e870-5ba4-8f44-69c9d5eac8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46cd9f15-1dee-5aee-b3d9-70903f558ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163111" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432338} {:answer/id #uuid "2b681e68-e901-5719-ac47-b49d4ce4701f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432341} {:answer/id #uuid "7d832a86-9407-57b1-9b8a-27976545a36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432351} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e42428-9e42-5652-97ed-add0db5753d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432354} {:answer/id #uuid "feef8743-284c-59a2-9f3c-29febdcbdf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1655fe4a-7777-5f50-b20e-15de50423a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432338} :answer-field/id #uuid "67008e00-3fcb-5aa6-b40b-a1c4a9012f7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c474ae22-f722-549c-93e7-7a344f22e1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163127" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3x+2y+{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+2x{y}^{3} & 2+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n6+5+(x-1)+(x-1)+6+(x-1)=3x+14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0889e045-221d-548d-8586-c263b14daf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278673} {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279071} {:answer/id #uuid "b804339b-81d0-5458-8b89-1119bfccd852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280210} {:answer/id #uuid "bec80d1d-dc4a-5247-8229-4f3da550f0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280269} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd330d2-e165-5873-970a-d719dc56d528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279071} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3902cd-4ea0-53f7-8e46-8bbcf944e194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0b608b5-0740-5c5c-a8d8-286478d0cfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163143" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x+y}+2x & \\frac{1}{x+y}+3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+2 & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+6y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+4+(y-1)+(y-1)+5+(y-1)=3y+7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d4caa4-4beb-5879-be0e-2ecd7839fde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+6e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278675} {:answer/id #uuid "673f89a5-8bcd-52d1-a51d-1e9b6862c990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279527} {:answer/id #uuid "8518450c-6a7d-5908-8248-9d4d5e973d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279786} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a9b9b-e6c8-5944-9c02-0f2375033954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+10e-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280570} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e66d77-2537-5e67-9679-68844748ea83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+10e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e00a9b9b-e6c8-5944-9c02-0f2375033954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+10e-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280570} :answer-field/id #uuid "55491170-a6a3-5d0f-aeb5-a0ffe8175fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a1651c-7c64-5ebe-93b5-ec0cfeb0b0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163144" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{3x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c81fe4bd-a0dc-58cb-a3c3-7221340209eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194706} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc44a84-faf0-5c88-a299-3f7b37efb1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194707} {:answer/id #uuid "1adc1ef2-f6a5-5e30-95da-4e8bbce99ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194708} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa4d4da-a09e-586d-9e45-a06d0144721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194709} {:answer/id #uuid "600f7255-9cdc-55b5-b56b-0668aa15e5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194710}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bedbaad-3c1a-55e5-8be0-38974a60928d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0de2219-f612-5500-b901-2bbe932a5e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367896} {:answer/id #uuid "afa2df40-c481-5ee8-89e4-3c7317d6dedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367897} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbccf2e-4992-5b52-aa5b-e32675c46f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367898} {:answer/id #uuid "55b524e7-7174-5848-9bd0-f0d9e4ce323e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367899} {:answer/id #uuid "0a588d3d-083b-5a5b-9835-8d329ae6113b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a588d3d-083b-5a5b-9835-8d329ae6113b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367900} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf900c1-0434-5330-ae18-cf1e1c43f585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fcb7ddf-9df6-5eb3-88f6-02683fa452c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-164809" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,1) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =2, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & ≈2+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30badc79-fc5e-5344-ad7c-8a9a4154d658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282124} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6b341-3aec-58bc-922c-d954e7bbad7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282457} {:answer/id #uuid "5c368add-a07b-5dd9-b643-d26d0eafbba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282470} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b77b3e-3ac4-5c5d-9953-5b9f183b497e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283396} {:answer/id #uuid "c871d9c5-8ad7-58fa-aa05-e4c911b10055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30badc79-fc5e-5344-ad7c-8a9a4154d658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282124} :answer-field/id #uuid "e583c6d9-dc1d-5b2d-9463-46e1326bfe44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e72817fe-f751-5c2a-9faa-b82f2365cc69" :question/math-academy-id "q-164828" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & ≈-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-e+\\begin{bmatrix}-e & e\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-e+2e+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{2}(-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :