2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2+1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(-4) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} {:answer/id #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281872} {:answer/id #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281934} {:answer/id #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282054} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf50d5a-a34b-5ce3-bd0f-8c170f17a78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d02b8fb-22de-53d2-a4aa-1c9d236c7ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b5cfc5-5b79-5869-9762-17001e7c7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432342} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed0217-d425-5f4e-ba99-e0ac900da7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432343} {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9aead5-698e-55fd-a7ed-c0810fdc4083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432355} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ec6153-7a5a-564c-b65e-6663c5889657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ace458-f233-535c-b685-fcd05334410a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164860" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1,-1) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(2,-1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(2,-1,-1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & ≈4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{1}{2}(-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
